.\" lumi.1 : manual page for lumiMUX
.\" Copyright (c) 2026 Jon Mayo
.\" Licensed under MIT-0 OR PUBLIC DOMAIN
.TH LUMI 1 "2026-10-05" "lumiMUX @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lumi \- terminal multiplexer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lumi
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ args ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lumi
is a terminal multiplexer with a git-style sub-command architecture.
Each sub-command can also be invoked directly as
.BI lumi- command
(e.g.\&
.BR lumi-new ).
.PP
If no command is given,
.B lumi
prints usage information and exits.
.SH COMMON OPTIONS
The following options are accepted by most sub-commands:
.TP
.BI \-s " name"
Session name.
Defaults to
.B 0
if not specified.
.TP
.BI \-m " mode"
UI mode:
.BR screen " (default),"
.BR turbo ,
.BR minimal .
Accepted by
.B new
and
.BR attach .
.SH COMMANDS
.SS lumi new
Create a new session and attach to it.
.PP
.B lumi new
.RB [ \-A ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ "\-f window" ]
.RB [ "\-m mode" ]
.RB [ "\-s name" ]
.RI [ shell ]
.TP
.B \-A
If the session already exists, reattach to it instead of failing.
The reattach takes the session over, the same as a plain
.BR "lumi attach" ;
there is no way to join a session from here, use
.B "lumi attach \-x"
for that.
.TP
.B \-d
Detach mode.
Create the session and start the server but do not attach.
.TP
.BI \-f " window"
Focus the given window (by server PID) after startup.
Passed through to
.BR lumi-attach .
.TP
.BI \-m " mode"
UI mode passed to
.BR lumi-attach .
.TP
.BI \-s " name"
Session name (default:
.BR 0 ).
.TP
.I shell
Shell command to run inside the new session.
Uses the user's login shell if omitted.
.SS lumi attach
Attach to an existing session.
.PP
.B lumi attach
.RB [ "\-f window" ]
.RB [ "\-m mode" ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-x ]
.RB [ \-F ]
.RB [ \-V ]
.RB [ "\-s name" ]
.RI [ name ]
.PP
By default an attach takes the session over: any client already attached is
told to detach, and this client gets the keyboard.
.PP
Several clients may be attached to one session at the same time instead.
.B \-x
asks for that, and so does
.BR \-v .
One of them holds the keyboard; the others watch.
The client that attaches first holds it, and it passes to another attached
client if that one leaves.
.B "lumi share \-M multi-writer"
lifts the one-keyboard rule for a session, and a session in that mode is
never taken over: a plain attach joins it as though
.B \-x
had been given.
.TP
.BI \-f " window"
Focus the given window (by server PID) after startup.
.TP
.BI \-m " mode"
UI mode:
.BR screen " (default),"
.BR turbo ,
.BR minimal .
.TP
.B \-v
Attach read-only.
The session is displayed and followed live, but nothing typed here reaches
it, and the window is neither resized nor killed by this client.
Scrollback, copy mode, the window list, and the menus all work as usual,
since they are local to this client.
This implies
.BR \-x .
.IP
A read-only client does not take part in deciding the window size: the
window is sized for the clients that can type, and a viewer with a smaller
terminal shows the part that fits and marks the taskbar with
.BR clip .
.B share.resize
changes this.
.IP
A read-only client also follows the client holding the keyboard: the window
it focuses and the layout it arranges.
.B \-F
turns the following off.
A client that is read-only only because someone else got there first keeps
its own view either way.
.TP
.B \-x
Share the session instead of taking it over.
The clients already attached stay attached, and this one asks for the
keyboard rather than taking it: in single-writer mode it gets the keyboard
only if nobody holds it, and otherwise attaches read-only and says so.
Use
.B "lumi share"
or the share menu to move the keyboard afterwards.
.IP
Unlike
.BR \-v ,
a client attached this way did not ask to watch, so it keeps its own focus
and layout rather than following the client holding the keyboard, and it is
given the keyboard as soon as the holder leaves.
.TP
.B \-F
Do not follow the client holding the keyboard.
This client chooses its own focused window and layout while it watches.
.TP
.B \-n
Connect through the session's
.B lumi net-proxy
over an encrypted netchan link instead of the local Unix sockets.
.RB "With a local " name ,
the proxy must already be running for the session (see
.BR "lumi net-proxy" );
the endpoint and pre-shared key are read from the
.I net-addr
and
.I net-key
files the proxy published in the session directory and dialed on the
loopback interface.
.RB "With an scp-style remote " name " (" host : session ),
.B lumi attach
starts the proxy on
.I host
over
.BR ssh (1)
with
.BR "lumi net-proxy \-d" ,
reads the endpoint and key it reports back through the ssh channel, and
then dials that endpoint directly.
Window I/O flows over the encrypted netchan link rather than through ssh.
.IP
The link roams: if the client's network changes, the session follows the
new address automatically without reattaching.
.TP
.B \-V
Verify the net-proxy's long-term identity key (requires
.BR \-n ).
The proxy must be started with
.BR "lumi net-proxy \-k" .
The server presents an X25519 identity key during the handshake; it is
compared against the copy the proxy published out of band (the
.I net-hostkey
session file for a local target, or a third field on the
.BR "lumi net-proxy \-d"
report line for a remote one) and against the client's
.I known_hosts
store under
.IR ~/.config/lumi .
On first contact the key's fingerprint is shown and confirmation is
requested before it is pinned; a later mismatch aborts the connection.
This authenticates the server across restarts, when the rotating
pre-shared key alone cannot.
.TP
.BI \-s " name"
Session name (default:
.BR 0 ).
A positional argument may also be used.
.PP
A session given as a
.B lumi://
URL is a direct-connect target: it dials a persistently-listening
.B "lumi net-proxy \-L"
and authenticates the user over netchan with no ssh bootstrap.
.PP
.RS 4
.BI lumi:// [ user @] host : port / session
.RE
.PP
.I user
defaults to
.BR $USER .
The connection is encrypted and the server's host key is pinned in
.I ~/.config/lumi/known_hosts
on first contact (with a fingerprint prompt), exactly as
.B \-V
does.
The user then logs in with the identity key from
.I ~/.config/lumi/id_netchan
(see
.BR "lumi net-keygen" ),
or, if that is refused or absent, a password.
An encrypted key is unlocked with a passphrase prompt before connecting.
.PP
Remote sessions may be specified using scp-style syntax:
.PP
.RS 4
.RI [ user @ ] host : session
.RE
.PP
When a remote session name is given,
.B lumi attach
launches
.B lumi proxy
on the remote host via
.BR ssh (1)
and tunnels all window I/O over the SSH connection.
If the session part is omitted (e.g.\&
.IR host: ),
it defaults to
.BR 0 .
Names beginning with
.B /
or
.B .
are always treated as local.
.SS lumi detach
Detach clients from a session, without attaching to it.
.PP
.B lumi detach
.RB [ "\-s name" ]
.RB [ "\-c id" ]
.PP
With no
.B \-c
every client attached to the session is told to detach, which is how a
session that has been left shared is made private again from another
terminal.
The session and the programs running in it are untouched; only the clients
watching it go.
.PP
Detaching is cooperative.
Each client acts on the request when it next reads the session directory,
so this waits a moment for them and reports any that did not go.
A client attached through a broker (a cross-user client, or
.BR "lumi attach \-n" )
does not read that directory and is not detached this way.
.TP
.BI \-s " name"
Session name (default:
.BR 0 ).
.TP
.BI \-c " id"
Detach just this client, by the id
.B "lumi share \-l"
prints.
The keyboard passes to another attached client if there is one.
.PP
This command moves clients, not settings.
A session left in multi-writer mode stays in it, and the next client to
attach shares it again rather than taking it over.
.B "lumi share \-M single\-writer"
turns that off, and
.B "lumi share \-L"
closes the session to further clients.
.SS lumi share
Show the clients attached to a session, and move the keyboard between them.
.PP
.B lumi share
.RB [ "\-s name" ]
.RB [ \-l ]
.RB [ "\-g id" ]
.RB [ "\-r id" ]
.RB [ "\-k id" ]
.RB [ "\-a id" ]
.RB [ "\-d id" ]
.RB [ \-L ]
.RB [ \-U ]
.RB [ "\-M mode" ]
.RB [ "\-D display" ]
.PP
.B lumi share
.RB [ "\-s name" ]
.RB "{" "\-u user" \||\| "\-G group" \||\| \-B "}"
.PP
By default one client at a time holds the keyboard; the rest watch.
Sharing has to be asked for, since a plain
.B "lumi attach"
takes the session over instead of joining it: see
.B \-x
and
.B \-v
under
.BR "lumi attach" .
.B \-M multi\-writer
lifts the one-keyboard rule, letting every attached client type (see
below).
The client ids printed by
.B \-l
are what the other options take.
.TP
.B \-l
List the attached clients: id, name, who (the uid or netchan key identity
behind a cross-user connection, blank for the session owner's own local
clients), role, UI mode, coupling, size, pending state, and how long each
has been attached, preceded by who holds the keyboard.
Coupling is
.B mirror
or
.B free
for a local view-role client (see
.B \-v
and
.B \-F
under
.BR "lumi attach" ),
and
.B \-
for a write-role client or a cross-user connection, neither of which the
concept applies to.
Pending is
.B yes
for a connection admitted under an
.B ask
rule that has not yet been approved or refused (see
.B "Cross-user access"
below), and
.B \-
otherwise.
This is the default when no other option is given.
.TP
.BI \-g " id"
Give the keyboard to this client.
The client holding it steps down without being asked.
.TP
.BI \-r " id"
Ask the client holding the keyboard to pass it to this client.
The holder is told, and answers from its share menu; the request is not
granted on its own.
.TP
.BI \-k " id"
Ask this client to detach.
.TP
.BI \-a " id"
Admit this pending client.
A connection matching an
.B ask
rule in
.I session\fR/\fBaccess
is held open, sent nothing, until admitted this way or refused with
.BR \-d .
See
.B "Cross-user access"
below.
.TP
.BI \-d " id"
Refuse this pending client.
Its connection is closed without ever having received any session
content.
.TP
.B \-L
Close the session to further clients.
An attach is refused while this is set.
Clients already attached are unaffected.
This guards against joining by accident, not against a client that ignores
it; for that, use the
.B lumi net-proxy
path and its key-based access control.
.TP
.B \-U
Open the session to further clients again.
.TP
.BI \-M " mode"
Set the session's write mode:
.B single\-writer
(the default) or
.BR multi\-writer .
In
.B multi\-writer
mode a client asking to type is granted it even while another client
already holds it; nobody already typing is demoted.
A session in this mode is also never taken over: a plain
.B "lumi attach"
joins it as though
.B \-x
had been given.
Takes effect for the next client that attaches, including a client that
reattaches; it does not retroactively change the role of a client already
attached, which stays as it was until it reattaches or is given the
keyboard with
.BR \-g .
.TP
.BI \-D " display"
Set the session's write-role layout coupling:
.B independent
(the default) or
.BR shared .
Only meaningful in
.B multi\-writer
mode.
.B independent
means a write-role client never applies another writer's layout or focus
changes to itself, though it still publishes its own; with
.B shared
every write-role client follows the others' layout and focus the same way
a mirror-coupled view-role client already does (see
.B \-v
under
.BR "lumi attach" ).
Takes effect for the next client that attaches, same timing as
.BR \-M .
.TP
.BI \-u " user"
Start the cross-user broker
.RB ( "lumi proxy \-L" )
for this session, granting the named local user
.B view
access.
If
.I session\fR/\fBaccess
does not already exist it is created (mode 0600, owner-only) seeded with
.RI "\(dq owner write\(dq, \(dq user:" user " view\(dq" ,
and \(dq* deny\(dq; an existing file is left untouched.
The broker listens on a Unix socket outside the session directory, and
authenticates every connection by the kernel-reported peer uid against
.IR session\fR/\fBaccess .
See
.B "Cross-user access"
below.
.TP
.BI \-G " group"
Start the cross-user broker granting the named group
.B view
access, seeded the same way as
.BR \-u ,
with a
.RI subject " group:" group
line.
The broker's socket is group-owned and mode 0660, so the kernel refuses a
non-member before the connection reaches lumi's own checks.
.TP
.B \-B
Stop the cross-user broker for this session.
Clients already bridged through it are unaffected until they detach; no
further client can connect.
.TP
.BI \-s " name"
Session name (default:
.BR 0 ).
.PP
Inside an attached client the same things are done from the share menu,
bound to
.B C\-a *
by default: it lists the clients, marks which one has the keyboard and
which one is this client, and shows who is asking for it.
.B Enter
gives the keyboard to the selected client, or asks for it when this client
does not have it,
.B k
asks the selected client to detach,
.B d
declines a pending request, and
.B l
closes or opens the session to further clients.
.SS lumi kill
Terminate a session.
.PP
.B lumi kill
.RB [ "\-s name" ]
.TP
.BI \-s " name"
Session name (default:
.BR 0 ).
.SS lumi list
List active sessions.
.PP
.B lumi list
.PP
Takes no options.
.SS lumi new\-window
Create a new window in an existing session.
.PP
.B lumi new\-window
.RB [ "\-s name" ]
.RI [ command " [" args ...]]
.TP
.BI \-s " name"
Session name (default:
.BR 0 ).
.TP
.IR command " [" args ...]
Program and arguments to run in the new window, executed directly (not
through a shell). With no command the window runs the user's login shell.
Option parsing stops at the command, so its own options are passed through
unchanged.
.SS lumi attr
Get, set, delete, or list per-session attributes.
.PP
.B lumi attr
.RB [ "\-s name" ]
.B get
.I key
.br
.B lumi attr
.RB [ "\-s name" ]
.B set
.I key value
.br
.B lumi attr
.RB [ "\-s name" ]
.B delete
.I key
.br
.B lumi attr
.RB [ "\-s name" ]
.B list
.TP
.BI \-s " name"
Session name.
Defaults to the
.B LUMI_SESSION
environment variable, or
.B 0
if unset.
.SS lumi splash
Display an ANSI art splash screen.
.PP
.B lumi splash
.RI [ scene ]
.RI [ name ]
.TP
.I scene
One of
.BR space ,
.BR mountain ,
or
.BR beach .
Chosen at random if omitted.
.TP
.I name
Text displayed on the splash screen (default:
.BR lumiMUX ).
.SS lumi files
Browse the filesystem in a full-screen terminal UI.
.PP
.B lumi files
.RI [ path ]
.PP
Starts in
.I path
or the current directory. The listing shows directories before files, with
a
.B ..
entry to move to the parent. Directory contents and a peek at text files
appear in a preview pane on terminals at least 64 columns wide.
.TP
.I path
Directory to open (default: the current directory).
.PP
Keys:
.RB [ j / k ]
or the arrow keys move the selection,
.RB [ g / G ]
jump to the first or last entry,
.RB [ Enter ]
or
.RB [ l ]
enters a directory or views a file in
.BR $PAGER ,
.RB [ e ]
edits the selected file in
.BR $EDITOR ,
.RB [ o ]
and
.RB [ O ]
open it in a new window of the current session (running
.BR $PAGER " or " $EDITOR )
when run inside
.BR lumi ,
.RB [ Backspace ]
or
.RB [ h ]
returns to the parent, and
.RB [ q ]
or
.B Esc
quits.
.PP
.RB [ Ctrl\-U ]
and
.RB [ Ctrl\-D ]
scroll the preview pane by half a screen, and
.RB [ . ]
toggles whether dot (hidden) files are listed.
.PP
Three operations change the filesystem.
.RB [ m ]
prompts for a name and creates a directory,
.RB [ r ]
renames the selection (the prompt is pre-filled with the current name), and
.RB [ d ]
deletes the selection after a yes/no confirmation. A directory is removed
only when empty. New names may not contain
.BR / ,
so an operation stays within the current directory.
.SS lumi edit
Edit a text file in a full-screen terminal UI.
.PP
.B lumi edit
.RI [ file ]
.PP
Opens
.I file
for editing, or an empty buffer when no path is given. A path that does not
exist opens as a new empty buffer saved to that name. The editor is
modeless by default: printable characters are inserted at the cursor.
Press
.RB [ F2 ]
for vi keybindings (described below).
.TP
.I file
File to edit (default: an unnamed empty buffer).
.PP
The screen is framed like the DOS
.B EDIT
program: a menu bar across the top, a window border with the file name
centered on it, scroll bars down the right edge and along the bottom, and a
status line showing key hints and the cursor
.IR Line : Col .
Press
.RB [ F10 ]
or
.RB [ Alt\- letter ]
to open a pull-down menu (File, Edit, Search, Build, View, Options, or Help);
the
.I letter
is the underlined mnemonic in each menu title.
The
arrow keys move between menus and items,
.B Enter
runs the highlighted item, and
.B Esc
closes the menu. Inside an open menu, typing an item's underlined mnemonic
letter runs that item directly without arrowing to it. Each item maps to work described below, and the File menu
also offers New, Open, and Save As. New, Open, and Exit put up a
Yes/No/Cancel dialog to save first when the buffer has unsaved changes.
The shortcut shown to the right of an item follows the active personality:
the modeless
.RB [ Ctrl\- letter ]
chords by default, and the equivalent vi keys (for example
.RB [ :w ]
for Save and
.RB [ :q ]
for Exit) while vi keys are on.
.PP
The mouse is enabled by default: click the menu bar to open a menu, click an
item to run it, click a line to place the cursor, drag to select, and use the
wheel to scroll.
.B Options
\(->
.B Mouse
turns mouse reporting off, which restores the terminal's own selection;
.B View
\(->
.B "Color Scheme"
switches between the blue DOS palette and a monochrome fallback. Toggle
items (Color Scheme, Syntax Highlight, Vi Keys, Mouse) show a bullet in the
menu when they are enabled.
.B Help
\(->
.B About
shows a modal box with the program name and version, dismissed by any key or
a click.
.PP
Keys: the arrow keys move the cursor,
.RB [ Home / End ]
go to the start or end of the line,
.RB [ PgUp / PgDn ]
scroll by a screen,
.RB [ Enter ]
splits the line,
.RB [ Backspace ]
and
.RB [ Delete ]
remove the character before or after the cursor and join lines at the ends,
.RB [ Ctrl-Z ]
and
.RB [ Ctrl-Y ]
undo and redo,
.RB [ Ctrl-S ]
saves (prompting for a name when the buffer has none), and
.RB [ Ctrl-Q ]
quits, prompting first when the buffer has unsaved changes.
.PP
Selection and clipboard: holding
.B Shift
with a movement key extends a selection, which is shown in reverse video.
.RB [ Ctrl-C ]
copies the selection to an internal clipboard, or the current line when
there is no selection.
.RB [ Ctrl-X ]
cuts the selection, and
.RB [ Ctrl-V ]
pastes the clipboard at the cursor, replacing the selection when one is
active.
.PP
Copying or cutting also mirrors the text to the terminal's system clipboard
with an OSC 52 escape, so it can be pasted into other applications;
terminals without OSC 52 support ignore it.
In reverse, bracketed paste inserts text pasted into the terminal literally
at the cursor, so control characters and newlines in it do not trigger
editor commands.
.PP
.RB [ Ctrl\-F ]
prompts for a string and moves the cursor to the next match, searching
forward from the cursor and wrapping to the top. The search is
case-sensitive, and pressing
.B Enter
on the pre-filled prompt repeats the last search.
.RB [ Ctrl\-L ]
prompts for a 1-based line number and jumps to it, clamping to the last
line.
.RB [ F1 ]
shows a full-screen list of the key bindings; any key returns to editing.
The list follows the active personality, showing the modeless bindings by
default and the vi bindings while vi keys are on. The status bar shows an
.RB [ F1 ]
hint when the editor opens.
.PP
.RB [ F2 ]
toggles a vi keybinding personality on top of the modeless editor. When it
is on the editor is modal, and the status bar shows the current mode. In
NORMAL mode the cursor rests on a character (shown as a block) and keys are
commands; in INSERT mode (shown as a bar) typing inserts text and
.B Esc
returns to NORMAL.
.RB [ F2 ]
in NORMAL mode returns to the modeless editor. Motions:
.RB [ h ]\ [ j ]\ [ k ]\ [ l ],
.RB [ 0 ]\ [ ^ ]\ [ $ ],
.RB [ w ]\ [ b ]\ [ e ]
(and
.RB [ W ]\ [ B ]\ [ E ]),
.RB [ gg ]
and
.RB [ G ],
with a numeric count (for example
.RB [ 3j ],
or
.RB [ 5G ]
and
.RB [ 5gg ]
to go to a line). A run of
.RB [ j ]/[ k ]
keeps aiming at the same display column across shorter lines, and
.RB [ $ ]
makes them stick to the end of each line.
.RB [ f ]\ [ F ]\ [ t ]\ [ T ]
search within the line for the next typed character (forward, backward,
and up to it), and
.RB [ ; ]
and
.RB [ , ]
repeat that search in the same or the opposite direction.
.RB [ { ]
and
.RB [ } ]
move by paragraphs (blank-line separated), and
.RB [ ( ]
and
.RB [ ) ]
move by sentences (ended by
.BR . ,
.BR ! ,
or
.B ?
followed by whitespace).
.RB [ % ]
jumps between a bracket
.RB ( () ,
.BR [] ,
or
.BR {} )
and its match, counting nesting; when the cursor is not on a bracket it
uses the first one further along the line. With a count,
.RB [ N% ]
instead jumps to
.I N
percent of the way through the file. An operator with an exclusive
motion that ends at the start of a later line drops that trailing line,
so
.RB [ d} ]
from the start of a paragraph deletes whole lines.
.RB [ H ]\ [ M ]\ [ L ]
move to the first, middle, and last line of the visible window (with a
count,
.RB [ H ]
and
.RB [ L ]
count in from the top or bottom).
.RB [ | ]
moves to a display column on the current line (column 1 without a count,
or the count-th column), accounting for tab stops and wide characters.
Entering insert:
.RB [ i ]\ [ a ]\ [ A ]\ [ I ]\ [ o ]\ [ O ].
Editing:
.RB [ x ]
deletes characters,
.RB [ p ]
and
.RB [ P ]
put the register after or before the cursor, and the operators
.RB [ d ]\ [ c ]\ [ y ]
apply to a following motion or a doubled key
.RB ( dd ,\ cc ,\ yy ).
.RB [ D ]
and
.RB [ C ]
delete or change to the end of the line,
.RB [ s ]
substitutes the character under the cursor and
.RB [ S ]
the whole line,
.RB [ ~ ]
toggles the case of the character (advancing past it), and
.RB [ J ]
joins the next line onto this one with a single space. A count applies
to each.
.RB [ r ]
followed by a character replaces the character under the cursor (a
count replaces that many, and
.B r
then Enter breaks the line), while
.RB [ R ]
enters Replace mode, where typing overwrites existing characters until
.BR Esc .
The operators
.RB [ > ]
and
.RB [ < ]
shift lines by one indent level (a tab), over a following motion or a
doubled key
.RB ( >> ,\ << );
a count multiplies the number of lines.
An operator may instead take a text object: an
.RB [ i ]
(inner) or
.RB [ a ]
(a, including surroundings) followed by
.B w
or
.B W
for a word,
a bracket
.RB ( "( ) b { } B [ ] < >" ),
or a quote
.RB ( \(dq
.B '
.BR ` ).
For example
.B diw
deletes the word under the cursor,
.B ci(
changes inside the enclosing parentheses, and
.B ya\(dq
yanks a double-quoted string with its quotes. The same
.RB [ i ]/[ a ]
objects select in visual mode
.RB ( viw ,\ va( ).
.RB [ m ]
followed by a letter
.RB ( a\(en z )
sets a mark at the cursor;
.RB [ ` ]
then that letter jumps back to the exact spot and
.RB [ ' ]
to the first non-blank of the mark's line. Either jump also works as a
motion for an operator
.RB ( d`a ,\ y'b ).
Marks hold absolute positions and do not shift as the buffer is edited.
.RB [ \(dq ]
followed by a letter selects a named register
.RB ( a\(en z )
for the next delete, yank, or put, so
.B "\(dqayy"
yanks a line into register a and
.B "\(dqap"
puts it back. An uppercase name
.RB ( A\(en Z )
appends to the register instead of replacing it. A delete or yank always
copies to the unnamed register too, which is what a plain
.RB [ p ]
uses.
.RB [ . ]
repeats the last change, whether an edit, an operator, or an insert
command with the text that was typed. It leaves marks, motions, yanks,
and undo alone.
.RB [ u ]
undoes and
.RB [ Ctrl-R ]
redoes a whole command at once.
.RB [ Ctrl-D ]/[ Ctrl-U ]
and
.RB [ Ctrl-F ]/[ Ctrl-B ]
scroll.
Visual mode:
.RB [ v ]
starts a charwise selection and
.RB [ V ]
a linewise one, both anchored at the cursor and extended by any motion. In
visual mode
.RB [ d ]
(or
.RB [ x ])
deletes the selection,
.RB [ y ]
yanks it,
.RB [ c ]
(or
.RB [ s ])
changes it, and
.RB [ p ]
replaces it with the register, each returning to NORMAL;
.RB [ o ]
jumps to the other end of the selection, and
.RB [ v ]/[ V ]
or
.B Esc
leaves visual mode. The status bar shows
.B -- VISUAL --
or
.BR "-- VISUAL LINE --" .
.RB [ ZZ ]
writes the buffer if it changed and quits;
.RB [ ZQ ]
quits without writing. A
.RB [ : ]
command line runs
.BR w ,
.BR q ,
.BR wq ,
.BR x
(write and quit),
.BR q! ,
.BR qa ,
.BR qa! ,
.BR wqa ,
.BR cq
(quit with a nonzero exit status), and
.BR :\fIN\fR
(go to a line).
A command may take a leading line range built from addresses
.RB ( N ,
.BR . ,
.BR $ ,
a mark
.BR '\fIx\fR ,
and
.BI +/- N
offsets),
.B %
for the whole file, or two addresses separated by a comma. Range commands
are
.B d
(delete),
.B y
(yank),
.B >
and
.B <
(shift), and
.BI s/ pat / rep /[ g ]
(substitute
.I pat
with
.I rep
on each line, the first match or, with
.BR g ,
all).
.BI g/ pat / cmd
runs
.I cmd
.RB ( d
or
.BR s )
on each matching line;
.B v
(or
.BR g! )
acts on the non-matching lines. Patterns are plain substrings, not regular
expressions.
Several files can be open at once:
.BI e\  path
opens a file in a new buffer (or switches to it when already open),
.B e
with no argument reloads the current file,
.B enew
starts an empty buffer,
.B ls
lists the buffers,
.B bn
and
.B bp
step to the next and previous,
.BI b\  N
switches to buffer
.IR N ,
and
.B bd
closes the current one
.RB ( bd!
to discard changes), and
.BI r\  path
reads a file in below the current line. The
.RB [ F8 ]
and
.RB [ Shift+F8 ]
keys step to the next and previous open file (also
.B File
\(->
.BR "Next Buffer" / "Prev Buffer" ), and
.B File
\(->
.B Buffer List
summarizes them. The top border shows a
.RB [ cur/total ]
indicator while more than one file is open.
.RB [ / ]
searches forward and
.RB [ ? ]
backward;
.RB [ n ]
repeats the last search in its own direction and
.RB [ N ]
in the opposite one.
.RB [ * ]
and
.RB [ # ]
search forward and backward for the word under the cursor (as a plain
substring, so they also match inside longer words).
.PP
Files whose extension names a known language (for example
.BR c ,
.BR h ,
or
.BR sh )
are syntax highlighted: comments, strings, numbers, keywords, types, and
preprocessor or shell variables are colored. The vi command
.B :syntax
takes
.BR off ,
.BR on ,
or a language name to disable, re-enable, or force highlighting for the
buffer.
.PP
The
.B View
menu's
.B Hex Dump
item toggles a hexadecimal view of the current buffer: an offset column, 16
bytes in hex, and an ASCII gutter with nonprintable bytes shown as a dot. It is
the same buffer as the text view, including any unsaved edits, so switching
back and forth keeps the cursor on the same byte. Arrow keys and
.RB [ PgUp ] / [ PgDn ]
move the cursor byte,
.B g
prompts for an offset (hexadecimal), and
.BR q " or " Esc
returns to the text view.
.RB [ Tab ]
switches between the hex and ascii columns.
.RB [ Ins ]
toggles between overwrite and insert editing; the status line shows
.B OVR
or
.BR INS .
In the hex column two hex digits edit a byte, and in the ascii column any
printable character does. Overwrite replaces the byte under the cursor. Insert
adds a new byte at the cursor, and a
.B 0a
byte typed in the hex column inserts an implied newline, splitting the line.
.RB [ Del ]
removes the byte under the cursor and
.RB [ Backspace ]
the byte before it; deleting an implied interior newline joins the two lines.
The single trailing newline of the file is not a deletable byte. Edits go
through the normal undo and dirty tracking, and
.B File
\(->
.B Save
writes the bytes back.
.PP
In the hex column,
.B /
prompts for text to find and
.B \e
for a hex-byte pattern (byte pairs, for example
.BR "0a 0d" );
the search wraps around the buffer and moves the cursor to the match.
.B n
and
.B N
repeat it forward and backward. The pattern may span an implied newline.
.B w
cycles the dump width between 8, 16, and 32 bytes per row; the wider rows
need a correspondingly wide terminal.
.B i
toggles a data-inspector line above the status bar that decodes the bytes at
the cursor: the first byte as unsigned and signed 8-bit and as a character,
then the 16- and 32-bit values in little-endian and big-endian order.
.B v
starts a byte selection at the cursor (press it again to clear it); moving the
cursor extends the selection.
.B y
copies the selected bytes, or the single byte under the cursor, to the
clipboard (also mirrored to the system clipboard), and
.B p
inserts the clipboard bytes at the cursor. A pasted newline splits the line.
Because the hex and text views edit the same buffer through the same commands,
.B Ctrl+S
saves and
.B Ctrl+Q
quits from the hex view exactly as from the text view, including the prompt to
save a modified buffer before exiting.
.PP
The
.B Build
menu runs a shell command chosen by the file's extension:
.RB [ F5 ]
runs the program (compile and run),
.RB [ F6 ]
compiles it, and
.RB [ F7 ]
runs
.B make
in the file's directory. The buffer is saved first unless configured
otherwise, and the command runs in the file's directory by default.
.RB [ F6 ]
and
.RB [ F7 ]
run captured: their output streams into a scrollable viewer as it is
produced, so the editor never freezes on a slow build. The view follows the
newest line until you scroll up with the arrow keys, and
.B q
cancels a running build. Any
.RI path : line
or
.RI path : line : col
diagnostics are collected into one list shared by all buffers. On return the
cursor jumps to the first diagnostic;
.RB [ F4 ]
and
.RB [ Shift+F4 ]
move to the next and previous one (also
.B Build
\(->
.BR "Next Error" / "Prev Error" ). A diagnostic in another file opens that
file in a new buffer, so navigation spans every file the build named.
.RB [ F5 ]
is interactive: inside a
.B lumi
session it is sent to another pane (the same target as
.RB [ Ctrl\-G ]),
otherwise the editor suspends, runs it, and waits for a key. The commands are
set per extension in
.I lumi.conf
(see the
.B [build]
configuration section), and built-in defaults cover
.BR make ,
compiling C, and running a shell or BASIC file when the config is silent.
.PP
When run inside a
.B lumi
session,
.RB [ Ctrl\-G ]
sends the selection, or the current line when nothing is selected, to
another window of the session as typed input, followed by a carriage
return so it runs there. See
.B lumi send\-input
for how the target window is chosen.
.SS lumi basic
A BASIC-style calculator that reads statements from standard input.
.PP
.B lumi basic
.RI [ file ]
.PP
With a
.I file
argument, the program in
.I file
is loaded and run before the prompt, then control drops to the REPL. This is
the
.B run
target of
.BR "lumi edit" 's
Build menu for a
.B .bas
file:
.RB [ F5 ]
launches
.B lumi basic
.I file
in a pane, which runs the program and leaves a REPL to inspect it.
.PP
Each line is one statement. A bare expression prints its value, so the tool
works as a calculator. Expressions use the usual operators with
.B ^
for exponentiation, comparisons that yield
.B 1
or
.BR 0 ,
and functions
.BR SIN ", " COS ", " TAN ", " ATN ", " SQR ", " EXP ", " LOG ", " ABS ", "
.BR INT ", and " SGN .
Variable names are case-insensitive and an unset variable reads as zero.
.PP
Values may also be vectors, written
.BR [1, " " 2, " " 3] ,
or matrices, written
.BR [[1, " " 2], " " [3, " " 4]] .
The operators
.BR + " " \- " and " *
work elementwise, with a scalar broadcast over every element, and a stored
vector or matrix is indexed
.RB ( 1 -based) as
.IR v ( i )
or
.IR m ( i , " " j ).
Functions
.BR ZEROS ", " RANGE ", " DOT ", " MATMUL ", and " LEN
build and combine them. Vectors and matrices are values, not variables to
assign into: there is no
.IR v ( i ) " = " x .
.PP
Statements:
.TP
.BI PRINT " item" ...
Print string literals and expression values, separated by
.B ,
(a space between) or
.B ;
(nothing between).
.TP
.BR LET " " \fIname\fR " = " \fIexpr\fR
Assign a variable. The
.B LET
keyword is optional.
.TP
.BR DEF " " \fIname\fR ( \fIparam\fR ) " = " \fIexpr\fR
Define a function of one parameter, called as
.IR name ( arg ).
By convention names begin with
.BR FN .
.TP
.BR PLOT " " \fIexpr\fR " FROM " \fIa\fR " TO " \fIb\fR
Draw an ASCII graph of
.I expr
as a function of
.I X
sampled across the range. With a vector argument,
.BI PLOT " v"
plots its elements instead.
.TP
.BI REM " text"
A comment.
.TP
.B QUIT
Leave the REPL.
.BR EXIT " and " BYE
do the same.
.PP
A line that begins with a number edits that
.I file
line of the stored program (1\-based) instead of being run: the numbered
text replaces that line, a number past the end appends a line, and a bare
number deletes the line. The program has no BASIC line numbers.
.B LIST
shows the program with a line\-number gutter (so you know which number to
edit),
.B NEW
clears it, and
.B RUN
executes it from the first line. A running program adds control flow:
.BR GOTO " " \fIlabel\fR ,
.BR GOSUB " " \fIlabel\fR " and " RETURN ,
.BR FOR " " \fIv\fR " = " \fIa\fR " TO " \fIb\fR " [STEP " \fIs\fR "] ... " NEXT ,
.BR IF " " \fIcond\fR " THEN " \fIstmt-or-label\fR ,
.BR INPUT " " \fIvar\fR ,
and
.BR END " (or " STOP ).
Jump targets are labels, not line numbers. A label is an identifier
followed by a colon at the start of a line
.RB ( "loop:" ),
alone or in front of a statement
.RB ( "loop: PRINT X" ),
and is matched without regard to case.
.B RUN
checks the program first and reports a duplicate label or a jump to an
undefined label, naming the file line, before running anything.
.PP
The program is managed from the prompt.
.BR SAVE " " \fIfile\fR
writes it out and
.BR LOAD " " \fIfile\fR
reads a program back, replacing the current one. These are prompt commands,
not language statements, so the interpreter itself does no file I/O.
.BR EDIT " [" \fIfile\fR ]
opens the program in
.B lumi edit
for full-screen editing, reads it back on exit, and then runs it
(save-and-run). With no
.IR file ,
the program goes through a temporary file that is removed afterward; it is
named with a
.B .bas
suffix so
.B lumi edit
highlights it as BASIC. With a
.IR file ,
that file is a persistent backing store: an existing non-empty file is edited
as it is, and a missing or empty one is first filled with the current program,
so
.B EDIT
.I file
both starts new files and reopens saved ones.
.B EDIT
needs a terminal.
.PP
When run inside a
.B lumi
session,
.BR SEND " " \fIexpr\fR
evaluates
.I expr
and sends its value to another window of the session, followed by a
carriage return. See
.B lumi send\-input
for how the target window is chosen.
.SS lumi version
Print version information.
.PP
.B lumi version
.PP
Takes no options.
.SS lumi reload
Reload server configuration.
.PP
.B lumi reload
.PP
Not yet implemented.
.SS lumi send\-keys
Send keystrokes to a session window, translating key names to their byte
sequences.
.PP
.B lumi send\-keys
.RB [ \-s
.IR session ]
.RB [ \-w
.I pid
|
.BI \-i " index"
|
.BR \-o ]
.RB [ \-l ]
.I key ...
.PP
Each argument is a key name, a modified key, or literal text, and the
translated bytes are sent as one atomic run to the target window, chosen
the same way as
.BR "lumi send\-input" .
Recognized key names (case-insensitive) are
.BR Enter ", " Return ", " Tab ", " Escape ", " Space ", " BSpace ", "
.BR Up ", " Down ", " Left ", " Right ", " Home ", " End ", " PPage ", "
.BR NPage ", " IC ", " DC ", and " F1 " through " F12 .
A key may be prefixed with
.B C\-
for Ctrl (on a single character) or
.B M\-
for Alt. Anything not recognized, and any argument under
.BR \-l ,
is sent verbatim. Arguments carry no implicit separator, so use
.B Space
or a quoted string for spaces, as in
.RB \(dq "send\-keys \(dqecho hi\(dq Enter" \(dq.
.TP
.BI \-s " session"
Session to target. Defaults to
.B $LUMI_SESSION
or
.BR 0 .
.TP
.BI \-w " pid"
Target the window with this micro-server process id.
.TP
.BI \-i " index"
Target the window with this number, the stable per-window number shown in
the status/tab bar and selected by the prefix plus a digit. Numbers are
0-based, and an out-of-range or empty number is an error.
.TP
.B \-o
Target a window other than the focused one (see
.BR "lumi send\-input" ).
.TP
.B \-l
Send every argument literally, without key-name translation.
.SS lumi send\-input
Inject raw bytes into a session window, as if they were typed.
.PP
.B lumi send\-input
.RB [ \-s
.IR session ]
.RB [ \-w
.I pid
|
.BI \-i " index"
|
.BR \-o ]
.RI [ bytes ...]
.PP
The bytes come from the arguments, joined with single spaces, or from
standard input when no argument is given. They are sent verbatim: no
newline is appended, so include a carriage return (for example from
.BR printf (1)
into standard input) to submit a line to a shell. The input is delivered
as one atomic run, so it cannot interleave with another client's typing.
.TP
.BI \-s " session"
Session to target. Defaults to
.B $LUMI_SESSION
or
.BR 0 .
.TP
.BI \-w " pid"
Target the window whose micro-server has this process id.
.TP
.BI \-i " index"
Target the window with this number, the stable per-window number shown in
the status/tab bar and selected by the prefix plus a digit. Numbers are
0-based, and an out-of-range or empty number is an error.
.TP
.B \-o
Target a window other than the focused one, preferring
.B $LUMI_SEND_TARGET
when it names a live window. This is what
.BR "lumi edit" 's
.RB [ Ctrl\-G ]
and
.BR "lumi basic" 's
.B SEND
use, so a tool in the focused window sends to the pane beside it.
.PP
Without
.BR \-w ,
.BR \-i ,
or
.BR \-o ,
the bytes go to the session's focused window. The input is injected even when
another client holds the keyboard, so a send reaches a pane in single-writer
mode without taking the keyboard from it. This is trusted because the command
runs as the session owner, whose sockets no other user can reach.
.SH INTERNAL COMMANDS
.SS lumi\-mserver
Single-PTY micro-server process.
Started automatically by
.BR "lumi new" " and " "lumi new\-window" ;
not intended for direct use.
.PP
.B lumi\-mserver
.B \-s
.I name
.RI [ shell ]
.TP
.BI \-s " name"
Session name (required).
.TP
.I shell
Shell command to run.
.SS lumi proxy
Multiplexing proxy for remote session tunneling.
Launched automatically by
.B lumi attach
for remote sessions; not intended for direct use.
The proxy connects to all micro-servers in the specified session,
multiplexes their I/O onto a single pipe (stdin/stdout),
and watches for new or removed windows.
.PP
.B lumi proxy
.B \-s
.I session
.TP
.BI \-s " session"
Session name (required).
.SS lumi net-proxy
Networked equivalent of
.B lumi proxy
that bridges a session over a reliable UDP (netchan) transport instead of
an SSH pipe.
It binds a UDP endpoint, publishes it as
.I net-addr
in the session directory, accepts one client, and multiplexes the I/O of
all micro-servers in the session over a single reliable channel.
.PP
The link is encrypted (X25519 handshake, XChaCha20-Poly1305 per packet).
The proxy mints a random pre-shared key at startup and publishes it as
.I net-key
in the session directory, readable only by the owner.
A client both encrypts with and authenticates against this key, so an
attacker who cannot read the session directory cannot connect.
Both files are removed when the proxy exits.
It is not launched automatically by
.BR "lumi attach" ;
connect to it with
.BR "lumi attach \-n" .
.PP
.B lumi net-proxy
.B \-s
.I session
.RB [ \-b
.IR addr ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-k ]
.TP
.BI \-s " session"
Session name (required).
.TP
.BI \-b " addr"
Bind address for the UDP endpoint (default: all interfaces).
.TP
.BI \-p " port"
UDP port to listen on (default: an ephemeral port, reported in
.IR net-addr ).
.TP
.B \-d
Daemon bootstrap for the cross-host attach path.
Print one line,
.RI \(dq "udp " port " " key \(dq,
on standard output reporting the bound port and the hex-encoded
pre-shared key, then detach and keep serving in the background.
With
.BR \-k ,
a third field carrying the hex-encoded host public key is appended.
.B lumi attach \-n
runs this over ssh to learn where and how to connect.
.TP
.B \-k
Present a long-term X25519 identity key so a client can verify the server
across restarts (see
.BR "lumi attach \-V" ).
The secret is loaded from, or generated on first use in,
.I ~/.config/lumi/host_key
(mode 0600, honoring
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ).
Its public half is published as
.I net-hostkey
in the session directory, and appended to the
.B \-d
report line.
Without this option the proxy authenticates by the pre-shared key alone.
.TP
.B \-L
Direct-connect mode: listen persistently on the fixed
.B \-p
port and bridge up to 16 clients at once (each in its own forked process,
one client per process as with
.BR "lumi proxy" ),
each authenticating over netchan with no ssh bootstrap.
There is no pre-shared key; the connection is encrypted, the server is pinned
by its host key (as with
.BR \-k ),
and the user proves identity with a public key or password.
The server reads
.I ~/.config/lumi/authorized_keys
(lines of
.RI \(dq user " " hexkey \(dq)
and
.I ~/.config/lumi/passwd
(Argon2id hashes); at least one must exist.
Connect with
.BR "lumi attach lumi://" user @ host : port / session .
A fixed
.B \-p
port is required.
.SS lumi net-keygen
Generate a netchan client identity key for user authentication over a
networked attach.
It writes an Ed25519 key pair, optionally sealed under a passphrase, and
prints the public key line to enroll in a server's
.I authorized_keys
file.
This is the client half of netchan's ssh-shaped userauth.
.PP
.B lumi net-keygen
.RB [ \-f
.IR keyfile ]
.RB [ \-N
.IR passphrase ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.TP
.BI \-f " keyfile"
Key path (default:
.IR ~/.config/lumi/id_netchan ,
honoring
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ).
The tool refuses to overwrite an existing key.
.TP
.BI \-N " passphrase"
Passphrase to seal the secret key with, stretched by Argon2id.
An empty value leaves the key unencrypted.
Without
.BR \-N ,
the tool prompts (with echo off) and asks for confirmation.
.TP
.B \-q
Print only the public key as hex, for scripting.
.SS lumi net-passwd
Enroll a password for a user on a direct-connect server, the password half
of netchan's userauth.
It reads a password from the terminal (with echo off, asked twice) and
appends an Argon2id hash for the named user to the server's
.I passwd
file.
.PP
.B lumi net-passwd
.RB [ \-f
.IR passwdfile ]
.I user
.TP
.BI \-f " passwdfile"
Password file path (default:
.IR ~/.config/lumi/passwd ,
honoring
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ).
The tool refuses a
.I user
that already has a line, so remove it first to change a password.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
All key bindings use a prefix key (default:
.BR Ctrl\-A )
followed by a second key.
Press
.B a
after the prefix to send a literal
.B Ctrl\-A
to the terminal.
.PP
.TS
l l.
Key	Action
_
c, C	Create new window
k, K	Kill current window
n, Space, Ctrl\-Space, Ctrl\-N	Next window
p, Ctrl\-P	Previous window
Ctrl\-A	Last window (most recently used)
0\-9	Select window by number
,	Swap number with next lower window
\&.	Swap number with next higher window
d, D	Detach from session
w, "	Window list
s	Toggle taskbar
q	Applications menu
[, Escape	Enter scrollback mode
h	Split pane horizontally
v	Split pane vertically
Tab	Next pane
X	Close pane
r	Resize pane
t	Toggle between turbo and screen modes
m	Minimize window (turbo mode)
f	Maximize window (turbo mode)
P	Window color picker (turbo mode)
G	Arrange windows in grid (turbo mode)
S	Toggle scroll lock
I	Toggle input lock
U	Session picker
]	Paste from internal clipboard
y	Sync internal clipboard to system
:	Open the command input line
l, Ctrl\-L	Redisplay (resync and repaint)
.TE
.PP
The
.B session picker
moves this client to another session.
Unlike
.BR "lumi attach" ,
it joins that session rather than taking it over: the clients already
there stay, and this one gets the keyboard only if nobody holds it.
Ask for the keyboard from the share menu, or take the session over by
attaching to it in the usual way.
.PP
Window numbers are stable: closing a window does not renumber the
others, so a closed window leaves a gap (a spare number) in the
sequence.
A newly created window takes the lowest free number.
Use
.B ,
and
.B .
to swap the current window's number with the next lower or higher
numbered window.
.PP
.B Next window
and
.B Previous window
step through windows in numeric order.
.B Last window
.RB ( "Ctrl\-A Ctrl\-A" )
instead jumps to the most recently used window, so pressing it
repeatedly toggles between the two most recent windows.
Closing the current window returns to the most recently used of the
windows that remain, rather than to the lowest-numbered one.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration file is loaded from:
.PP
.RS 4
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lumi/lumi.conf
.RE
.PP
or, if
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is not set:
.PP
.RS 4
.I $HOME/.config/lumi/lumi.conf
.RE
.PP
The file is optional; a missing file is silently ignored.
.SS Format
The file uses a git-config-style INI format.
Section headers are enclosed in brackets.
Keys and values are separated by
.BR = .
Lines starting with
.B #
or
.B ;
are comments.
Inline comments (after a value) are also supported.
Double-quoted values preserve literal
.B #
characters.
.PP
Dotted shorthand may be used outside a section block:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
taskbar.position = bottom
.fi
.RE
.PP
is equivalent to:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[taskbar]
position = bottom
.fi
.RE
.SS Keys
.TP
.B attach.mode
Default UI mode:
.BR screen " (default),"
.BR turbo ,
.BR minimal .
Overridden by the
.B \-m
command-line flag.
.TP
.B attach.altscreen-scrollback
When
.BR true ", " yes ", or " 1 ,
content shown by full-screen applications (those using the alternate
screen, such as editors and pagers) is copied into the window's scrollback
when the application exits, so
.B Ctrl\-A [
can review it.
Trailing blank lines are trimmed.
Default: off; applications that toggle the alternate screen often can leave
confusing partial history.
.TP
.B keys.prefix
Change the prefix key.
Accepts
.BR C\- \fIx\fR
notation or a named key (see
.B Key Names
below).
Default:
.BR C\-a .
.TP
.B taskbar.format
Taskbar format string.
Supports variable expansion and built-in functions.
.IP
When the session directory watch is unavailable, the taskbar is
prefixed with a fixed
.B [!watch]
marker.
This marker means
.B lumi attach
could not subscribe to live window changes (for example, the system ran
out of
.BR inotify (7)
instances).
Windows you create yourself still appear, but windows created or closed
by other clients attached to the same session will not update live until
the next rescan.
The marker is shown independently of
.B taskbar.format
so the degraded state is always visible.
.IP
While more than one client is attached, the taskbar is likewise prefixed
with a presence marker, by default
.BR share:1w+1v :
the number of clients holding the keyboard and the number watching.
It is drawn independently of
.B taskbar.format
as well.
.B share.indicator
changes how it reads; nothing turns it off.
The marker is drawn in a distinct color whenever at least one attached
client is not the session owner's own uid (a client relayed through
.B "lumi proxy \-L"
or
.BR "lumi net\-proxy \-L" ).
This coloring cannot be turned off either.
In
.B minimal
mode, which has no taskbar, the terminal title carries
.B [shared]
instead.
.TP
.B share.resize
Whether a read-only client takes part in deciding the window size:
.BR observe " (default) or " negotiate .
With
.BR observe ,
the window is sized for the clients that can type and a read-only client
crops what does not fit.
With
.BR negotiate ,
every attached client is counted and the window is sized to fit the
smallest of them, which is what
.BR screen (1)
does with
.BR \-x .
.TP
.B share.indicator
Format of the presence marker described above.
.B %w
expands to the number of clients holding the keyboard,
.B %v
to the number watching, and
.B %n
to the total.
Default:
.BR share:%ww+%vv .
.TP
.B taskbar.position
Taskbar position:
.BR top " or " bottom .
Default:
.BR top .
.TP
.B taskbar.shell_allow
Comma-separated
.BR fnmatch (3)
patterns for shell commands allowed in taskbar
.B $(shell ...)
expansions.
.TP
.B ui.theme
Name of the theme to use for turbo mode window decorations.
.SS Environment defaults
Keys in an
.B [environment]
section set environment variables for the shell started in a window and
for every program run inside it.
A variable is exported only if it is not already set in the environment
that started the session, so an inherited value always wins.
This gives programs a default, for example a
.B DISPLAY
value for a session started without one:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[environment]
DISPLAY = :0
EDITOR = vi
.fi
.RE
.PP
The defaults are applied when the session server starts, before the first
shell is forked.
They do not affect an already-running session.
.SS Build commands
The
.B Build
menu of
.B lumi edit
looks up its command per file extension in
.RB [ build " \(dq" ext \(dq]
subsections, falling back to a
.B [build]
default section and then to built-in defaults:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[build "c"]
    compile = cc -Wall -c $(filename)
    run     = ./$(filebase)
[build]
    make = make
.fi
.RE
.PP
Each verb key is
.BR compile ,
.BR make ,
or
.BR run .
A
.B dir
key sets the working directory (default: the file's own directory), and a
.B save
key of
.BR no ", " false ", or " 0
skips saving the buffer before building.
The command may use
.BR $(file) " (the full path),"
.BR $(filedir) ", " $(filebase) " (name without extension),"
.BR $(filename) " (name with extension), and " $(fileext) .
.SS Editor appearance
The
.B lumi edit
chrome (the editing area, the window frame, and the menu and status bars) uses
the DOS EDIT palette by default. An
.B [edit]
section can derive that palette from a named color theme instead:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[edit]
    theme = turbo
.fi
.RE
.PP
The theme names are the same as the window themes used elsewhere, for example
.BR turbo ", " thin ", " crimson ", or " acid .
The editing area and frame take the theme's content and border colors; the
bars invert the content colors. An unknown or missing name keeps the built-in
default.
.PP
Syntax highlighting colors are set separately in an
.B [edit.syntax]
section. Each key names a highlight style and its value is a color:
.BR default ", a palette index " 0 - 255 ", or " #rrggbb .
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[edit.syntax]
    comment  = 244
    keyword  = 3
    string   = #66cc66
.fi
.RE
.PP
The style keys are
.BR text ", " comment ", " keyword ", " type ", " constant ", " string ,
.BR operator ", " function ", and " preproc .
An unset key keeps the built-in color, and a bad value is ignored.
.SS Bindings
Key bindings are declared in a
.B [bind]
section.
Each entry maps a key name to an action name.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[bind]
b = prev\-window
.fi
.RE
.PP
To unbind a key, set its action to
.BR none .
.PP
Named binding layers activate based on predicates and are
declared with a subsection name:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[bind "vi"]
match\-title = ^vim
j = next\-window
k = prev\-window
.fi
.RE
.PP
Layer directives:
.TP
.B match\-title
POSIX extended regular expression matched against the window title.
The layer activates when the focused window title matches.
.TP
.B toggle
Named toggle.
The layer activates when the toggle is enabled.
.SS Key Names
Key names used in
.B keys.prefix
and
.B [bind]
entries:
.TP
.BI C\- x
Control key.
.I x
may be
.BR a \- z ,
.BR A \- Z ,
or
.BR @ \- _ .
.TP
.B space
Space character.
.TP
.B tab
Tab key.
.TP
.BR escape ", " esc
Escape key.
.TP
.B backspace
Backspace (DEL, 0x7f).
.TP
.B quote
Double-quote character.
.TP
.I c
Any single ASCII character (e.g.\&
.BR a ", " 0 ", " [ ).
.SS Action Names
.TS
l l.
Action	Description
_
send\-prefix	Send literal prefix key
new\-window	Create new window
next\-window	Next window
prev\-window	Previous window
select\-0 \- select\-9	Select window by number
swap\-num\-lower	Swap number with next lower window
swap\-num\-higher	Swap number with next higher window
kill\-window	Kill current window
detach	Detach from session
window\-list	Show window list
taskbar\-toggle	Toggle taskbar
apps\-menu	Applications menu
split\-h	Split horizontally
split\-v	Split vertically
next\-pane	Next pane
prev\-pane	Previous pane
close\-pane	Close pane
resize\-pane	Resize pane
scrollback	Enter scrollback mode
window\-colors	Window color picker
minimize	Minimize window
maximize	Maximize window
toggle\-mode	Toggle between turbo and screen modes
scroll\-lock	Toggle scroll lock
input\-lock	Toggle input lock
session\-list	Session picker
paste	Paste from internal clipboard
clipboard\-sync	Sync internal clipboard to system
command	Open the command input line
arrange\-grid	Arrange windows in grid (turbo mode)
redisplay	Resync and repaint the screen
share\-menu	Attached clients; pass the keyboard
none	Unbind key
.TE
.SH SCROLLBACK MODE
Enter scrollback mode with
.B Ctrl\-A [
or
.BR "Ctrl\-A Escape" .
Scrollback mode presents a movable copy cursor and vi-style keyboard
selection.
.TP
.BR "h" ", " "j" ", " "k" ", " "l" ", arrows"
Move the copy cursor left, down, up, and right.
Moving past the top or bottom edge scrolls the history.
.TP
.BR "0" ", " "$"
Move to the start or end of the line.
.TP
.BR "g" ", " "G"
Jump to the oldest history line or the live bottom.
.TP
.BR "Page Up" ", " "Page Down" ", " "Ctrl\-U" ", " "Ctrl\-D"
Move a half page up or down.
.TP
.BR "v" ", " "V" ", " "Ctrl\-V"
Begin a character, line, or rectangular block selection at the copy
cursor.
Movement then extends the selection.
.TP
.B Space
Set the selection mark, or, if a selection is active, copy it and exit.
.TP
.BR y ", " Enter
Copy the selection to the internal clipboard and exit scrollback mode.
.TP
.B Escape
Cancel an active selection, or exit scrollback mode when none is active.
.TP
.B Mouse wheel
Scroll up and down.
.TP
.B Mouse drag
Select text.
Selected text is copied to the internal clipboard on mouse release.
.TP
.B q
Exit scrollback mode.
.PP
A scrollbar is displayed on the right edge of the window in turbo mode.
Selection highlighting is constrained to the visible window bounds; a
keyboard selection is likewise limited to the currently visible screen.
Use
.B Ctrl\-A ]
to paste the copied text into the focused window.
.SH COMMAND LINE
.B Ctrl\-A :
opens a one-line command prompt at the bottom of the screen, similar to
the same binding in GNU screen.
Type a directive and press Enter to run it, or Escape to cancel.
The following directives are recognized:
.TP
.BI setenv " VAR value"
Set an environment variable used when creating new windows.
The value may be double-quoted to include spaces
.RI ( "setenv G \(dqhello world\(dq" ).
Existing windows are unaffected.
.TP
.BI unsetenv " VAR"
Remove a variable from the environment used for new windows.
.TP
.BI title " text"
Override the current window's displayed title with
.IR text .
The override is client-side and takes precedence over the title the
running program sets via OSC 0/2, so it does not get clobbered when the
program updates its own title.
The text may be double-quoted to include leading or trailing spaces.
Run
.B title
with no argument to clear the override and let the program's live
title show through again.
.TP
.BI number " N"
Renumber the current window to the stable window number
.IR N .
If another window already holds
.IR N ,
the two windows exchange numbers; otherwise the window moves into that
slot and its old number becomes a spare.
.B renumber
is an accepted alias.
See also
.B Ctrl\-A ,
and
.B Ctrl\-A .
which bump the current window's number to the adjacent slot.
.PP
An unrecognized or malformed directive leaves the prompt open with an
error message; any key dismisses it.
.SH MOUSE
In
.B turbo
mode, mouse interactions include:
.TP
.B Title bar drag
Move window.
.TP
.B Border drag
Resize window.
.TP
.B Close button click
Close window.
.TP
.B Click
Focus window and deliver click to the application.
.PP
In all modes, mouse text selection copies to the internal clipboard.
Double-click selects a word; triple-click selects a full line.
Dragging after a double- or triple-click extends by word or line units.
A visible selection is dismissed when the foreground window changes,
when scrollback mode is entered or left, and when windows are moved or
rearranged, since it is pinned to screen coordinates.
Use
.B Ctrl\-A ]
to paste and
.B Ctrl\-A y
to sync the internal clipboard to the system clipboard.
.PP
Bracketed paste sequences from the outer terminal are forwarded to
the focused window when the application has enabled bracketed paste
mode (DECSET 2004).
.PP
Inside a terminal emulator the mouse works out of the box using the
terminal's own reporting. On the raw Linux text console the terminal
reports no mouse events; there
.B lumi
reads them from the
.BR gpm (8)
daemon instead, when built with libgpm support.
.SH TERMINAL COMPATIBILITY
.B lumi
sets the host terminal's title to reflect the current window title.
Title changes from child applications (via OSC 0 and OSC 2) are
propagated to the outer terminal immediately.
.PP
Window layout (positions, sizes, z-order) is saved on detach and
restored on reattach to the same session.
.PP
The following terminal features are recognized and forwarded:
.TP
.B Kitty keyboard protocol
The prefix key (Ctrl\-A) is recognized in kitty keyboard encoding
(CSI 97;5u) in addition to the traditional byte encoding.
.TP
.B Keyboard enhancement protocols
Enhancement flags (XTPUSHSGR, kitty progressive enhancement) set by
child applications are forwarded from the micro-server to the outer
terminal.
.TP
.B DCS pass-through
SIXEL graphics and other DCS sequences are passed through to the
outer terminal when a single pane is focused in screen mode.
.TP
.B Desktop notifications
Notification escape sequences emitted by child applications
(kitty OSC 99, the simple OSC 9 form, and OSC 777) are passed through
to the outer terminal so pop-ups reach the user, regardless of which
window is in the foreground.
.TP
.B System clipboard (OSC 52)
A child application's OSC 52 set-clipboard request is forwarded to the
outer terminal, so copying inside a window (for example with
.BR "lumi edit" )
reaches the system clipboard. Read requests
.RB ( "OSC 52 ... ;?" )
are not forwarded, since their reply has no window to route back to.
The clipboard payload is capped at 256 KB.
.TP
.B Cursor shape
DECSCUSR (CSI Ps SP q) cursor shape changes are forwarded.
.SH SECURITY
.B lumi
sets
.BR umask (2)
to 077 at startup so that the runtime directory
.RI ( $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/lumi/ )
and its Unix domain sockets are accessible only by the owning user.
The original umask is restored for child shell processes.
.PP
The session directory is cleaned of stale entries (dead server
processes) on each attach and list operation.
.PP
When
.B "lumi net-proxy \-k"
and
.B "lumi attach \-V"
are used, the server's identity secret
.RI ( ~/.config/lumi/host_key )
is created with mode 0600 and the client pins server keys in
.IR ~/.config/lumi/known_hosts .
A direct-connect listener
.RB ( "lumi net-proxy \-L" )
also authenticates the user against
.I ~/.config/lumi/authorized_keys
and
.IR ~/.config/lumi/passwd ,
and the client presents its
.I ~/.config/lumi/id_netchan
key (mode 0600, optionally passphrase-sealed).
All paths honor
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME .
.SS Cross-user access
An mserver accepts a connection only from its own uid; a session directory
is mode 0700.
A different local user reaches a session only through the broker started by
.B "lumi share \-u"
or
.BR "\-G" ,
which runs as the session owner, checks the connecting peer's
kernel-reported uid and gid, and relays.
.PP
The broker's policy is
.IR session\fR/\fBaccess ,
mode 0600 and owned by the session's own uid; a file that fails either
check is treated as absent (an unreadable or wrongly moded ACL means
owner-only, not open access).
Lines are
.RI \(dq subject \ role\ [ask]\(dq ,
matched top to bottom with an implicit final deny; the first match wins.
Subjects are
.BR owner ,
.BI user: name-or-uid ,
.BI group: name-or-gid ,
.BI key: name
(a netchan client identified by keystore key, for
.BR "lumi net-proxy" ),
and
.BR * .
The role is a ceiling, not a grant: a
.B write
subject that attaches asking for
.B view
gets
.BR view .
A line whose subject cannot be parsed matches every peer, so a typo can
only take access away, never grant it; a line whose subject matches but
whose role cannot be parsed denies that match rather than falling through
to a more permissive line below it.
Every decision is appended to
.IR session\fR/\fBaccess.log
(mode 0600): timestamp, identity, the subject that decided it, the
requested role, and the granted role.
The
.B ask
option admits a match pending approval rather than granting or refusing it
outright: the connection is held open and sent nothing, not even the
initial window list, until the write-token holder or an owner client
approves it with
.B "lumi share \-a"
or refuses it with
.BR "lumi share \-d" .
If no client could plausibly answer (no write-token holder and no locally
attached owner client), or none does within a short timeout, the match is
denied, the same fail-closed default as a plain
.B deny
rule.
An approved connection is granted exactly the role the matching
.B ask
rule's role column named; nothing renegotiates a higher role afterward.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory for
.B lumi
configuration
.RI ( lumi.conf )
and the network identity files
.RI ( host_key ", " known_hosts ).
Defaults to
.IR ~/.config .
.TP
.B LUMI_SESSION
Set inside an attached session to the current session name.
Prevents recursive attach.
Used by
.B lumi attr
as the default session name when
.B \-s
is not given.
.TP
.B LUMI_SEND_TARGET
Process id of a window to prefer when
.B lumi send\-input \-o
(and the
.BR "lumi edit" / basic
send bindings) pick a window other than the focused one.
.TP
.B LUMI_LIBEXEC_PATH
Override the search path for
.BI lumi- command
executables.
.TP
.B LUMI_DEBUG
Set to a file path to enable debug tracing.
Diagnostic output is appended to the specified file.
.TP
.B PAGER
Program used by
.B lumi files
to view a file (default:
.BR less ).
.TP
.B EDITOR
Program used by
.B lumi files
to edit a file (default:
.BR vi ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I @PREFIX@/bin/lumi
The multi-call binary.
.TP
.I @PREFIX@/bin/lumi\-*
The per-command executables
.RB ( lumi-attach ", " lumi-new ", " ...),
found on
.B PATH
or through
.BR LUMI_LIBEXEC_PATH .
.TP
.I ~/.config/lumi/lumi.conf
Per-user configuration
.RB ( XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is honored).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR screen (1),
.BR tmux (1),
.BR ssh (1)
.SH AUTHORS
Jon Mayo
